## Summary

This draft PR adds a correctness-first CPU execution path for GLM-5.3-Flash / GLM5Next KDA in #57346. CPU platform dispatch selects `vllm.models.glm5next.cpu.kda`; the common layer uses CPU state gather/scatter helpers instead of CUDA-only Triton helpers.

The CPU backend preserves the existing GLM KDA contract: bounded per-key-dimension gating, q/k normalization, V-major recurrent state, packed `cu_seqlens`, ordinary state slots, NULL slot handling, speculative state routing, and prefill-to-decode state handoff. The independent eager reference remains in the test module and is not used by the backend.

The first native operator is an AVX-512 FP32 recurrent operator registered as `_C.glm5next_kda_recurrent`. Python dispatch uses it for the proven dense-state safe-gate contract and retains the PyTorch path for unsupported variants. Existing CPU causal convolution and `FusedRMSNormGated` paths are reused. GLM5Next now has a feature-local PyTorch speculative convolution wrapper implementing the accepted-token rolling-window contract; generic causal-conv code is unchanged.

## Validation

On the server CPU environment, with the real `_C_AVX512` extension loaded:

- focused GLM5Next KDA suite: `27 passed, 14 warnings`;
- FP32/BF16 native parity and state routing are covered by the suite;
- CPU import binding resolves both KDA symbols and state helpers to `vllm.models.glm5next.cpu.kda`;
- a production-shaped `Glm5NextLinearAttention` packed-prefill smoke crosses CPU causal convolution, CPU gather/scatter, native KDA, and cache writeback;
- prefill-to-decode and speculative layer smokes consume and advance cached state;
- server `ruff check`, `ruff format`, and local Python compilation / `git diff --check` passed.

The server tests use the documented `torchcodec` import shim because that environment's unrelated CUDA-built torchcodec library cannot load with CPU PyTorch. Full GLM-5.3-Flash E2E remains dependent on #57345's sparse MLA / KeyPool CPU backend. AMX/A
